# Copy to `.env.local` for development and set the same values wherever this
# site is deployed. `.env.local` is git-ignored.
#
# RFQ ("request a quote") submissions from /contact are emailed via SMTP.
# With none of SMTP_HOST/SMTP_USER/SMTP_PASS set, the form still accepts
# submissions but nothing is delivered — the API answers
# {ok:true, delivered:false} and the form tells the visitor to call or LINE
# instead of falsely promising a callback. No database, no third-party
# analytics, no CAPTCHA are used for this form — see PrivacyPage.tsx.

# ── SMTP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sends through the company's own mail server.
SMTP_HOST=mail.vaninter.com
SMTP_PORT=465
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
#
# The company mail host (same host the sibling VAN site uses) currently
# serves the stock self-signed certificate it shipped with (CN=localhost,
# issued to itself), so TLS validation cannot succeed and sending fails
# without the line below. It keeps the connection encrypted but
# UNAUTHENTICATED: a man-in-the-middle on the path could read the mailbox
# password and the lead. Proper fix: ask the mail host to install a valid
# certificate, then delete this variable. (See VAN/.env.example for the
# same note on the sibling site.)
SMTP_TLS_INSECURE=true

# ── Optional overrides ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Inbox that receives RFQ requests. Defaults to company.contact.email
# (src/data/company.ts).
# RFQ_TO_EMAIL=van@vaninter.com

# ── Test-only switch — never set this in production ──────────────────────
# Routes the API through nodemailer's jsonTransport (no network connection,
# composes the message and returns it) instead of real SMTP, so
# `npm run test:rfq` can exercise the real route code path without
# credentials. See scripts/test-rfq.mjs.
# RFQ_TEST_JSON_TRANSPORT=1
